  "headline": "On people who watched Mad Men: 7,582,850 US viewers in 2025 and 6,941,827 in 2026 through Nov 9, a loss of 641,023 viewers, with the remaining audience younger and slightly less affluent, Millennials up from 37.5% to 39.1% and $100,000-plus income down from 48.9% to 47.3%.",
  "reads": [
   "The contraction is concentrated at the older end. Viewers 55 and older fall from 32.1% of the audience to 30.7%, and 65 or older drops from 948,364 people to 832,283. The title skews toward people who came to it on streaming rather than on its original run, and that cohort leans 25 to 54.",
   "Affluence softens but does not break. The $250,000-or-more and $150,000-to-$249,999 bands both give back share while $25,000-to-$74,999 picks it up, which reads as the audience widening past the original coastal professional core rather than losing it: New York still indexes at 261 and San Francisco at 264.",
   "Geography is tilting out of the legacy top four. New York, Los Angeles, Chicago and San Francisco together move from 37.7% to 36.7% of viewers, while Dallas Ft Worth, Atlanta, Houston and Denver all gain share. That is the pattern of a catalog title finding incremental audience in lower-cost metros.",
   "Engagement is rising even as reach falls. The daily signal moves from an engagement score of 50.0 to 57.8 across 32 tracked days through Sep 14 2026, with unique people peaking at 149 a day on Aug 24 2026 and the latest read at 117 a day. A smaller audience is talking about the show more, which reads as a core that is consolidating rather than drifting."
  ],
